#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:1 of
msgid ""
"A gear joint constrains the rotational speeds of two rigid bodies. It "
"ensures that the two bodies rotate relative to each other at a fixed "
"ratio, simulating the mechanical link of a gear system or a belt drive."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:6 of
msgid ""
"The first Mobject to be connected. Typically represents the driving or "
"reference gear."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:9 of
msgid ""
"The second Mobject to be connected. Its rotation is linked to `a_mob` "
"based on the defined `ratio`."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:12 of
msgid ""
"The angular offset (in radians) between the two bodies. Adjusts the "
"initial relative orientation alignment."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:15 of
msgid ""
"The gear ratio. Defines how the angular velocity of `b_mob` relates to "
"`a_mob`. For example, a ratio of 2.0 means `b_mob` rotates twice as fast "
"as `a_mob`."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:19 of
msgid ""
"The class used to visualize the rotational direction or connection "
"(defaults to `Arrow`). If set to `None`, no indicator will be rendered."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:22 of
msgid ""
"A dictionary defining the visual style of the indicator line, including "
"`color` and `stroke_width`."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGearJoint.VGearJoint:25 of
msgid "The visual length of the indicator arrows drawn on each Mobject."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:1 of
msgid "Initializes a Groove Joint constraint between two Mobjects."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:3 of
msgid ""
"A Groove Joint constrains a point on the second body to a line segment "
"(the \"groove\") on the first body. The groove is defined by two points "
"relative to the first body's center, and the anchor point is relative to "
"the second body's center."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:9 of
msgid "The Mobject that contains the groove (the rail/track)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:11 of
msgid "The Mobject that contains the sliding anchor (the slider)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:13 of
msgid "The start point of the groove, in `a_mob`'s local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:15 of
msgid "The end point of the groove, in `a_mob`'s local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:17 of
msgid ""
"The anchor point on `b_mob` that slides within the groove, in local "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:19 of
msgid "Visual Mobject representing the start of the groove."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:21 of
msgid "Visual Mobject representing the end of the groove."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:23 of
msgid "Visual Mobject representing the sliding anchor on the second body."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:25 of
msgid "The Manim class used to draw the groove line (e.g., Line or DashedLine)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VGrooveJoint.VGrooveJoint:27 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the groove line."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:1 of
msgid ""
"A pin joint keeps a fixed distance between two anchor points on two rigid"
" bodies. It acts like a solid, weightless rod connecting two points, "
"allowing them to rotate freely around the anchors while maintaining the "
"specified `distance`."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:10 of
msgid ""
"The local anchor point on `a_mob` where the pin is attached, relative to "
"the Mobject's center."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:13 of
msgid ""
"The local anchor point on `b_mob` where the pin is attached, relative to "
"the Mobject's center."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:16 of
msgid ""
"The fixed distance to maintain between the two anchors. If `None`, it is "
"automatically calculated as the initial distance between anchors."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:19 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject used to visually represent the anchor point on `a_mob` (e.g.,"
" a red `Dot`)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:22 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject used to visually represent the anchor point on `b_mob` (e.g.,"
" a red `Dot`)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint:25 of
msgid ""
"The class used to draw the connecting rod (e.g., `Line`). Defaults to "
"`None` for no visible connection."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPinJoint.VPinJoint.install:1 of
msgid ""
"Create the Pymunk PinJoint constraint, initialize visual anchor markers "
"and connecting line, add the constraint to the space, and register the "
"per-frame updater."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:1 of
msgid ""
"A pivot joint allows two rigid bodies to rotate freely around a common "
"pivot point. The pivot point acts as an axis of rotation, ensuring that "
"both bodies remain attached at the specified position regardless of "
"external forces."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:6 of
msgid "Please configure pivot_world or (anchor_a_loca, anchor_b_local)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:13 of
msgid ""
"The global coordinate position of the pivot point. If provided, "
"`anchor_a_local` and `anchor_b_local` are calculated automatically based "
"on this."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:16 of
msgid ""
"The local anchor point on `a_mob` corresponding to the pivot, relative to"
" the Mobject's center."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:19 of
msgid ""
"The local anchor point on `b_mob` corresponding to the pivot, relative to"
" the Mobject's center."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:22 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject used to visually represent the anchor point on `a_mob` "
"(defaults to a red `Dot`)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:25 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject used to visually represent the anchor point on `b_mob` "
"(defaults to a red `Dot`)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:28 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject used to visually represent the pivot point (defaults to a "
"white `Dot` with 0.05 radius)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:31 of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to draw the line connecting the anchors to the pivot"
" (e.g., `Line`). Pass `None` to disable the connecting lines."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VPivotJoint.VPivotJoint:34 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the connecting lines."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:1 of
msgid "Initializes a Ratchet Joint constraint between two Mobjects."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:3 of
msgid ""
"A Ratchet Joint acts like a rotary pawl, allowing relative rotation only "
"in discrete increments and in one direction. It is perfect for simulating"
" winding mechanisms, clockwork, or unidirectional gears."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:8 of
msgid "The first Mobject (the base or reference body)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:10 of
msgid "The second Mobject (the body subject to the ratchet effect)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:12 of
msgid "The initial angular offset of the ratchet teeth."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:14 of
msgid ""
"The angular distance between each \"tooth\" (in radians). For example, "
"PI/2 means the joint locks at every 90-degree interval."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:17 of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to visualize the current rotation or ratchet "
"direction."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:19 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the indicator arrow."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:21 of
msgid "The visual length of the indicator line."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:23 of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to draw a line between the centers of the two "
"objects. Set to None to disable."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint:26 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the connection line."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRatchetJoint.VRatchetJoint.install:1 of
msgid ""
"Create the Pymunk RatchetJoint constraint, initialize visual indicator "
"arrows and connecting line, add to the space, and register the per-frame "
"updater."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:1 of
msgid "Initializes a Rotary Limit Joint constraint between two Mobjects."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:3 of
msgid ""
"This joint constrains the relative rotation between two bodies to stay "
"within a specific angular range. It acts as a physical stop when the "
"bodies reach the minimum or maximum angle limits."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:8 of
msgid "The first Mobject (the reference body)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:10
#: of
msgid "The second Mobject (the constrained body)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:12
#: of
msgid "The minimum allowed relative angle (in radians)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:14
#: of
msgid "The maximum allowed relative angle (in radians)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:16
#: of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to visualize the angular limits (typically Arc). Set"
" to None to hide the visual representation."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint:19
#: of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the visual arc."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VRotaryLimitJoint.VRotaryLimitJoint.install:1
#: of
msgid ""
"Create the Pymunk RotaryLimitJoint constraint, initialize arc indicator "
"visuals, add the constraint to the space, and register the per-frame "
"updater."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:1 of
msgid "Initializes a Simple Motor constraint between two Mobjects."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:3 of
msgid ""
"A Simple Motor maintains a constant relative angular velocity between two"
" bodies. It applies the necessary torque to reach and maintain the target"
" rotation rate, up to a defined maximum torque limit."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:8 of
msgid "The first Mobject (often the base or stator)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:10 of
msgid "The second Mobject (often the rotor or driven part)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:12 of
msgid "The target relative angular velocity in radians per second."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:14 of
msgid ""
"The maximum torque the motor can apply to achieve the target rate. "
"Setting this to a finite value allows the motor to \"stall\" under load."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:17 of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to visualize the rotation (e.g., Arrow or "
"CurvedArrow)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor:19 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the visual indicator."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSimpleMotor.VSimpleMotor.install:1 of
msgid ""
"Create the Pymunk SimpleMotor constraint, set max torque, initialize "
"visual indicator, add to the space, and register the per-frame updater."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:1 of
msgid "Initializes a Slide Joint constraint between two Mobjects."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:3 of
msgid ""
"A Slide Joint holds two bodies between a minimum and maximum distance. It"
" acts like a solid link when the distance reaches the limits, but allows "
"free movement within the specified range."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:8 of
msgid "The first Mobject to connect."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:10 of
msgid "The second Mobject to connect."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:12 of
msgid "The anchor point on the first body, defined in local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:14 of
msgid "The anchor point on the second body, defined in local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:16 of
msgid "The minimum allowed distance between the two anchor points."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:18 of
msgid "The maximum allowed distance between the two anchor points."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:20 of
msgid "The visual representation (Mobject) of the first anchor point."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:22 of
msgid "The visual representation (Mobject) of the second anchor point."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:24 of
msgid ""
"The Manim class used to draw the connection line (e.g., Line or "
"DashedLine). Pass None to disable the visual indicator."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint:27 of
msgid "Configuration dictionary for the styling of the indicator line."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.constraints.VSlideJoint.VSlideJoint.install:1 of
msgid ""
"Create the Pymunk SlideJoint constraint, initialize visual indicator line"
" and anchor markers, add to the space, and register the per-frame "
"updater."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:1 of
msgid ""
"Pymunk physical space management is generally not used by users. This "
"object has already been created in SpaceScene."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:4 of
msgid "The Manim visualization manager for a Pymunk physical space."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:6 of
msgid ""
"The VSpace class encapsulates the Pymunk "
":class:`~manim_extensions.physics.rigid_mechanics.rigid_mechanics.Space` "
"object, managing rigid bodies, shapes, and constraints within the "
"simulation. It utilizes Manim's updater mechanism to synchronize the "
"physical simulation results with Mobject visual states in real-time."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:12 of
msgid "The gravity acceleration vector $(g_x, g_y)$. Defaults to $(0, -9.81)$."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:14 of
msgid ""
"The number of sub-steps per frame for physical simulation. Increasing  "
"this value improves numerical stability and collision accuracy. Defaults "
"to 8."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:15 of
msgid "The number of sub-steps per frame for physical simulation. Increasing"
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:16 of
msgid "this value improves numerical stability and collision accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:17 of
msgid "Defaults to 8."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace:19 of
msgid "Forwarded to the parent :class:`~manim.mobject.mobject.Mobject`."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.init_updater:1 of
msgid ""
"Register the per-frame step updater that advances the physics simulation "
"and synchronizes Mobject positions with their bodies."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.remove_body_shapes_constraints:1
#: of
msgid "Removes physical bodies, shapes, or constraints from the physical space."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.remove_body_shapes_constraints:3
#: of
msgid ""
"This method handles the unregistration of Pymunk objects. It is crucial "
"for maintaining simulation performance and preventing memory leaks or "
"unexpected physical interactions after a Mobject has been removed from "
"the scene."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.remove_body_shapes_constraints:8
#: of
msgid ""
"The Pymunk objects (Body, Shape, or Constraint) to be removed from the "
"simulation space."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:1 of
msgid "Sets up both the physical body and its collision shapes for a Mobject."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:3 of
msgid ""
"This method acts as a high-level initializer that configures the motion "
"properties (velocity, gravity center) and the physical material "
"properties (friction, elasticity) simultaneously, effectively binding a "
"complete physical identity to a visual Mobject."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:9 of
msgid "The Mobject to be initialized with physical properties."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:11 of
msgid "The Pymunk body type (Dynamic, Static, or Kinematic)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:13 of
msgid "Whether the shapes are treated as solid objects or hollow boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:15 of
msgid ""
"The coefficient of restitution. Controls how much energy is preserved "
"after a collision."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:17 of
msgid "The friction coefficient. Controls how much the object resists sliding."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:19 of
msgid ""
"The density used to calculate mass and moment of inertia based on shape "
"area."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:21 of
msgid ""
"If True, the shapes will trigger collision callbacks but won't cause "
"physical bounces."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:23 of
msgid ""
"A constant velocity applied to the surface of the shape (e.g., for "
"conveyor belts)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:25 of
msgid "The center of mass relative to the Mobject's center $(x, y)$."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:27 of
msgid "The initial linear velocity vector $(v_x, v_y)$."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_body_and_shapes:29 of
msgid "The initial angular velocity in radians per second."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_local_point:1 of
msgid ""
"Applies a force to a Mobject's physical body at a point defined in local "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_local_point:3 of
msgid ""
"The force is applied relative to the body's current orientation. If the "
"point is not the center of gravity, it will also generate a torque, "
"causing the object to rotate."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_local_point:8
#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_world_point:9 of
msgid "The Mobject whose physical body will receive the force."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_local_point:10 of
msgid ""
"The force vector $(f_x, f_y, f_z)$ to apply. Note that Pymunk operates in"
" 2D, so the z-component is typically ignored."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_local_point:13 of
msgid ""
"The offset from the body's center of gravity $(x, y, z)$ where the force "
"is applied, in local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_world_point:1 of
msgid ""
"Applies a force to a Mobject's physical body at a point defined in world "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_world_point:3 of
msgid ""
"The force vector is applied at an absolute position in the scene. If the "
"point does not coincide with the body's center of gravity, it will "
"generate torque and cause the body to rotate. This is useful for external"
" influences that occur at specific scene locations."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_world_point:11 of
msgid ""
"The force vector $(f_x, f_y, f_z)$ to apply. Note that Pymunk typically "
"ignores the z-component."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_force_at_world_point:14 of
msgid ""
"The absolute position in the world (scene) coordinates where the force is"
" applied. Defaults to the origin $(0, 0, 0)$."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_local_point:1
#: of
msgid ""
"Applies an instantaneous impulse to a Mobject's physical body at a local "
"point."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_local_point:3
#: of
msgid ""
"Impulses cause an immediate change in velocity (linear and angular) "
"without requiring time to elapse, simulating effects like a sudden hit or"
" explosion. The point is defined relative to the body's current position "
"and orientation."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_local_point:8
#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_world_point:9
#: of
msgid "The Mobject whose physical body will receive the impulse."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_local_point:10
#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_world_point:11
#: of
msgid ""
"The impulse vector $(i_x, i_y, i_z)$ to apply. The z-component is "
"typically ignored in 2D physics."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_local_point:13
#: of
msgid ""
"The offset from the body's center of gravity $(x, y, z)$ where the "
"impulse is applied, in local coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_world_point:1
#: of
msgid ""
"Applies an instantaneous impulse to a Mobject's physical body at a world "
"coordinate."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_world_point:3
#: of
msgid ""
"The impulse vector is applied at an absolute position in the scene. This "
"causes an immediate change in the body's linear and angular velocity. If "
"the application point is offset from the body's center of mass, the body "
"will begin to rotate."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.apply_impulse_at_world_point:14
#: of
msgid ""
"The absolute position in world (scene) coordinates where the impulse is "
"applied. Defaults to the origin $(0, 0, 0)$."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.local_to_world:1 of
msgid ""
"Convert a point from the Mobject's local body coordinates to world "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.local_to_world:9 of
msgid "The corresponding world-space point (x, y, 0)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.world_to_local:1 of
msgid ""
"Convert a point from world coordinates to the Mobject's local body "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.world_to_local:9 of
msgid "The corresponding local-space point (x, y, 0)."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_position_func:1 of
msgid "Assigns a custom position update callback to a Mobject's physical body."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_position_func:3 of
msgid ""
"By default, Pymunk updates a body's position based on its velocity. This "
"method allows you to override that behavior with custom logic. The "
"callback is executed during every physical simulation step."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_position_func:8 of
msgid ""
"The Mobject whose physical body's position update logic will be "
"customized."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_position_func:10 of
msgid ""
"A function with the signature `def callback(body: pymunk.Body, dt: "
"float)`. If None, the default Pymunk position update logic is restored."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_velocity_func:1 of
msgid "Assigns a custom velocity update callback to a Mobject's physical body."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_velocity_func:3 of
msgid ""
"This method overrides how Pymunk calculates velocity in each step. It is "
"commonly used to implement specialized physical effects such as custom "
"air resistance (drag), planetary gravity, or specific damping behaviors "
"that differ from the global space settings."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_velocity_func:8 of
msgid "The Mobject whose physical body's velocity logic will be customized."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.set_velocity_func:10 of
msgid ""
"A function with the signature: `def callback(body: Body, gravity: "
"Tuple[float, float], damping: float, dt: float)` If None, the default "
"Pymunk velocity update logic is restored."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.velocity_at_world_point:1 of
msgid ""
"Return the velocity of a point on the Mobject's body expressed in world "
"coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.get_point_query_info:1 of
msgid ""
"Performs a spatial query to find the relationship between a point and a "
"Mobject's shapes."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.get_point_query_info:3 of
msgid ""
"This method calculates how a specific point in space relates to the "
"physical boundaries of a Mobject. It is essential for determining if a "
"point is inside an object, how far it is from the surface, and the "
"direction to the closest surface point."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.get_point_query_info:9 of
msgid "The Mobject whose associated physical shapes will be queried."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.get_point_query_info:11 of
msgid ""
"A (x, y, z) coordinate representing the test location in the scene. Note:"
" Only the (x, y) components are used for the 2D physics engine."
msgstr ""

#: manim_extensions.pymunk.space.VSpace.VSpace.get_point_query_info:14 of
msgid ""
"A list of tuples, where each tuple contains:  .. list-table::     "
":header-rows: 0      * - ``distance``       - The distance from the point"
" to the shape (negative if inside).     * - ``gradient``       - A 3D "
"vector representing the direction of the distance gradient.     * - "
"``point``       - The closest point on the shape's surface to the query "
